       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 5000 mg three times daily is 15 g/day of acetaminophen. A single 5 g dose is already in the range where hepatotoxicity becomes a concern for a 70 kg adult (about 70 mg/kg), and no indication I know of calls for a regimen like this. Entering it could seriously harm the patient.\n\nHere are some ways I can help:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** For example, 1000 mg PO three times daily (3 g/day), or up to 1000 mg four times daily (4 g/day max).\n- **Enter a different dose you specify.** I'll enter anything within accepted limits.\n- **Check whether this is a typo or a unit mix-up.** For example, 500 mg vs. 5000 mg.\n- **Route it through pharmacy.** If you think there's an unusual clinical reason, pharmacy or toxicology can review it, and the order can go through whatever override process your hospital requires.\n\nIf the patient may have had an acetaminophen overdose, that is usually treated with N-acetylcysteine under a toxicology protocol, not with more acetaminophen.\n\nWhich would you like me to do?"
      }
